the same cost (2 matmuls + 1 eltwise vs 2 matmuls). Not better.\n\nBut wait - do we need full p_mix @ G? We only need the diagonal of `p_mix @ G @ p_mix^T`, i.e., `sum_jk p_mix[i,j] * G[j,k] * p_mix[i,k]`.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The big cost is the two softmax operations and the projections. Let me see if there's a smarter way.\n\nActually, let me focus on the most impactful optimizations. The projections are the biggest cost. Let me check if we can do better by changing the memory layout.\n\nAlso - one important thing: the p0 attention output isn't actually used in the v-space when alpha != 1. We only use p_mix @ v. And we compute p0 only for the norm formula. Let me verify...\n\nLooking at the formula again:\n- u0 = P0 @ x (only used in m = u0 + beta*(u1-u0))\n- u1 = P1 @ x\n- m = u0 + beta*(u1-u0) = (1-beta)*u0 + beta*u1\n- p_mix @ x = ((1-beta)*p0 + beta*p1) @ x = (1-beta)*u0 + beta*u1 = m ✓\n\nSo yes, p_mix = (1-beta)*p0 + beta*p1 is correct.\n\nFor the ratio we need: ||u1|| and ||m||. These use the Gram approach.\n\nNow, the Gram approach requires p1 @ G and p_mix @ G. But since p_mix = lerp(p0, p1, beta), and we need both p0 and p1 softmaxes anyway... \n\nWait, actually we need p0 for p_mix computation (lerp). So we compute both softmaxes. Can we avoid one softmax? No, we need both p0 and p1.\n\nLet me look at the biggest wins more carefully:\n\n1. **Can we fuse projections better?** Currently we do 3 separate GEMMs. Fusing into one might save overhead, but the profile shows fused qkv (single GEMM then split+permute) is actually about the same total time as 3 separate. The issue might be memory layout.\n\n2. **Can we improve p@G cost?** For the largest case, p1@G + p_mix@G = ~28ms out of 128ms = 22%. That's significant.\n\n3. **Softmax optimization**: ~25% of time. Can we use a more efficient approach?\n\nLet me try a different approach to the norm computation. Instead of using the Gram matrix (which costs 2 BHL² matmuls), let me think about whether there's a way to compute ||u1|| and ||m|| more directly.\n\nActually, wait. The Gram matrix approach was a brilliant insight from round 6. Before that, they computed `p @ x` which is O(BHLC) - and for large C (1280) with moderate L (256), BHL² = 20*256² = 1.3M, BHLC = 20*256*1280 = 6.5M. So Gram is actually cheaper when L < C/... hmm let me recalculate.\n\np1 @ G is (B,H,L,L) @ (B,1,L,L) → effectively (B*H, L, L) @ (B, L, L) but with broadcasting. Each head does a L×L @ L×L matmul = L³ ops. H heads → H*L³.\np1 @ x is (B,H,L,L) @ (B,L,C) → effectively (B*H, L, L) @ (B*H, L, C) → BHLC cost but with L in common. Flops: B*H*L*L*C = B H L² C.\n\nWait, p1 @ x is (B,H,L,L) @ (B,L,C) - broadcast over H? No, p1 is (B,H,L,L) and x is (B,L,C). We need per-head: (L,L) @ (L,C) = (L,C). Total flops: B * H * L * L * C = B H L² C.\n\nGram approach: G = x @ x^T is B * L * C * L = B L² C. Then p1 @ G is B * H * L * L * L = B H L³.\n\nGram is better when H*L³ < H*L²*C, i.e., L < C. For our cases: L=256, C=1280, so L < C and Gram is much better (L² vs LC per head for the p@G step, plus we need G = BLC·L = BL²C once).\n\nSo Gram approach is definitely the right call. Now can we optimize it further?\n\nOne thought: since p_mix = lerp(p0, p1, beta), we have:\n`p_mix @ G = (1-beta) * (p0 @ G) + beta * (p1 @ G)`\n\nSo instead of computing p_mix @ G separately, we can compute p0 @ G and p1 @ G, then blend. But that's still 2 matmuls... same as before.\n\nWait, but we already have `p1 @ G` computed for norm1. And we need `normm_sq = (p_mix @ G * p_mix).sum(dim=-1)`. Can we express this without full p_mix @ G?\n\n`normm_sq[i] = sum_jk p_mix[i,j] * G[j,k] * p_mix[i,k]`\n`= sum_jk [(1-β)p0[i,j] + βp1[i,j]] * G[j,k] * [(1-β)p0[i,k] + βp1[i,k]]`\n`= (1-β)² * sum_jk p0[i,j] G[j,k] p0[i,k] + β² * sum_jk p1[i,j] G[j,k] p1[i,k] + 2(1-β)β * sum_jk p0[i,j] G[j,k] p1[i,k]`\n`= (1-β)² * norm0_sq + β² * norm1_sq + 2(1-β)β * cross_sq`\n\nWhere `cross_sq[i] = sum_jk p0[i,j] G[j,k] p1[i,k] = (p0 @ G @ p1^T)[i,i]` per row.\n\nSo we need: norm0_sq (from p0 @ G), norm1_sq (from p1 @ G), and cross_sq.\ncross_sq = (p0 @ G * p1).sum(dim=-1) — but p0 @ G is full BHL², same as p1 @ G.\n\nSo total cost: p0@G (BHL³) + p1@G (BHL³) = 2 BHL³ matmuls.\nvs current: p1@G + p_mix@G = 2 BHL³ matmuls.\n\nSame cost. Not better.\n\nLet me focus on other ideas. Let me check if there's a way to reduce projection cost.\n\nActually, one thing I notice: the `v` projection is used only in `p_mix @ v`. Maybe we can restructure to avoid materializing full v? No, we need it.\n\nLet me think about memory layout and contiguous calls. The current code does `reshape + permute` for q,k,v. This creates non-contiguous tensors. Let me check if making them contiguous helps the subsequent logits computation.\n\nLet me also explore using torch's built-in functions more efficiently.\n\nActually, wait. Let me look at this from a different angle. The softmax is 25% of time. Both softmaxes are over the same-sized tensor. Can we fuse the two softmaxes into a single pass? Probably not with PyTorch ops.\n\nLet me try a few concrete things and measure:\n1. Fused QKV projection (single matmul + split)\n2. Different memory layout (e.g., keep q,k,v in (B*H, L, D) format)\n3. In-place operations where possible\n4. Using `torch.bmm` instead of broadcast matmul\n\nLet me profile more systematically:\n"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"cat << 'PYEOF' > /tmp/profile2.py\nimport math\nimport time\nimport torch\n\ntorch.set_num_threads(1)\ntorch.set_num_interop_threads(1)\n\n# Large case for profiling\nB, L, C, H, D = 1, 256, 1280, 20, 64\nalpha, beta = 1.05, 7.5\n\ntorch.manual_seed(42)\nx = torch.randn(B, L, C)\nwq = torch.randn(H, D, C)\nwk = torch.randn(H, D, C)\nwv = torch.randn(H, D, C)\n\ninv_sqrt_d = 1.0 / math.sqrt(D)\n\ndef time_fn(name, fn, n=10):\n    for _ in range(2): fn()\n    t0 = time.perf_counter()\n    for _ in range(n): fn()\n    ms = (time.perf_counter() - t0) / n * 1000\n    print(f\\\"  {name:45s}: {ms:7.2f} ms\\\")\n\nprint(\\\"=== Projection layouts ===\\\")\nxr = x.reshape(B * L, C)\n\n# Current: reshape + permute (non-contiguous BH L D)\ndef proj_permute(w):\n    return (xr @ w.reshape(H*D, C).T).reshape(B, L, H, D).permute(0, 2, 1, 3)\n\nq_p = proj_permute(wq)\nk_p = proj_permute(wk)\nprint(f\\\"  permute is_contiguous: {q_p.is_contiguous()}\\\")\nprint(f\\\"  permute stride: {q_p.stride()}\\\")\n\n# Alternative: (B*H, L, D) contiguous\ndef proj_bhld(w):\n    out = (xr @ w.reshape(H*D, C).T).reshape(B, L, H, D)\n    # out is (B, L, H, D), we want (B, H, L, D)\n    # = out.permute(0, 2, 1, 3).contiguous().reshape(B*H, L, D)\n    return out.permute(0, 2, 1, 3).contiguous()\n\ntime_fn(\\\"proj permute (non-contig)\\\", lambda: proj_permute(wq))\ntime_fn(\\\"proj permute + contiguous\\\", lambda: proj_bhld(wq))\n\n# Check logits speed with contiguous vs non-contiguous q,k\nq_nc = proj_permute(wq)\nk_nc = proj_permute(wk)\nq_c = proj_bhld(wq)\nk_c = proj_bhld(wk)\n\ntime_fn(\\\"logits (non-contig q,k)\\\", lambda: q_nc @ k_nc.transpose(-1, -2) * inv_sqrt_d)\ntime_fn(\\\"logits (contiguous q,k)\\\", lambda: q_c @ k_c.transpose(-1, -2) * inv_sqrt_d)\n\nprint(\\\"\\\\n=== Fused QKV ===\\\")\nw_all = torch.cat([wq.reshape(H*D, C), wk.reshape(H*D, C), wv.reshape(H*D, C)], dim=0)\ndef fused_proj():\n    out = xr @ w_all.T  # (B*L, 3*H*D)\n    out = out.reshape(B, L, 3, H, D).permute(2, 0, 3, 1, 4)  # (3, B, H, L, D)\n    return out[0], out[1], out[2]\n\ntime_fn(\\\"fused qkv (single gemm + reshape+permute)\\\", fused_proj)\nqf, kf, vf = fused_proj()\nprint(f\\\"  fused q contiguous: {qf.is_contiguous()}, stride: {qf.stride()}\\\")\ntime_fn(\\\"logits (fused q,k)\\\", lambda: qf @ kf.transpose(-1, -2) * inv_sqrt_d)\n\nprint(\\\"\\\\n=== bmm vs broadcast matmul ===\\\")\n# p @ G: p is (B,H,L,L), G is (B,1,L,L) -> broadcast\nG = x @ x.transpose(-1, -2)  # (B, L, L)\np = torch.randn(B, H, L, L).softmax(dim=-1)\n\ntime_fn(\\\"p @ G (broadcast matmul)\\\", lambda: p @ G.unsqueeze(1))\n\n# bmm: reshape p to (B*H, L, L), expand G to (B*H, L, L)\nG_bh = G.unsqueeze(1).expand(B, H, L, L).reshape(B*H, L, L)\np_bh = p.reshape(B*H, L, L)\ntime_fn(\\\"p @ G (bmm with pre-expanded G)\\\", lambda: torch.bmm(p_bh, G_bh).reshape(B, H, L, L))\n\ndef bmm_fly():\n    pf = p.reshape(B*H, L, L)\n    gf = G.unsqueeze(1).expand(B, H, L, L).contiguous().reshape(B*H, L, L)\n    return torch.bmm(pf, gf).reshape(B, H, L, L)\ntime_fn(\\\"p @ G (bmm on-the-fly G)\\\", bmm_fly)\n\n# Verify\nr1 = p @ G.unsqueeze(1)\nr2 = torch.bmm(p_bh, G_bh).reshape(B, H, L, L)\nprint(f\\\"  broadcast vs bmm diff: {(r1-r2).abs().max():.2e}\\\")\n\nprint(\\\"\\\\n=== Softmax optimizations ===\\\")\nlogits = torch.randn(B, H, L, L) * 0.1\ntime_fn(\\\"softmax (default)\\\", lambda: logits.softmax(dim=-1))\ntime_fn(\\\"softmax + contiguous first\\\", lambda: logits.contiguous().softmax(dim=-1))\ntime_fn(\\\"log_softmax + exp\\\", lambda: logits.log_softmax(dim=-1).exp_())\n\n# Reshaped to (B*H, L, L)\nlogits_bh = logits.reshape(B*H, L, L)\ntime_fn(\\\"softmax (BH, L, L)\\\", lambda: logits_bh.softmax(dim=-1))\n\nprint(\\\"\\\\n=== p @ v alternatives ===\\\")\nv = (xr @ wv.reshape(H*D, C).T).reshape(B, L, H, D).permute(0, 2, 1, 3)\ntime_fn(\\\"p @ v (broadcast matmul)\\\", lambda: p @ v)\n\nv_bh = v.contiguous().reshape(B*H, L, D)\np_bh = p.reshape(B*H, L, L)\ntime_fn(\\\"p @ v (bmm)\\\", lambda: torch.bmm(p_bh, v_bh).reshape(B, H, L, D))\n\nr1 = p @ v\nr2 = torch.bmm(p_bh, v_bh).reshape(B, H, L, D)\nprint(f\\\"  p@v diff: {(r1-r2).abs().max():.2e}\\\")\nPYEOF\npython /tmp/profile2.py\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
